  {
   "slug": "what-a-cue-sheet-is-for",
   "title": "What a cue sheet is for",
   "date": "2026-10-14",
   "categories": [
    "notes"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "crew"
   ],
   "image": "assets/images/demo/rehearsal.webp",
   "excerpt": "How minute-by-minute cue notation prevents miscommunication during live plenary sessions.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A cue sheet is one page that every department reads from. It lists what happens, in what order, who calls it and who acts on it: the lighting change, the roll-in of the slide deck, the lectern microphone going live. Tomasz Okafor, our stage manager, calls it the only document that is allowed to be wrong in pencil and never wrong in print.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>On show day the sheet is not a script, it is an agreement. Every line carries a time, a trigger and a name. When the presenter runs two minutes long, the sheet tells the sound desk which cue moves and tells the lighting desk which one does not. Nobody has to ask over the headset, because the answer was written on Tuesday.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We print it on paper even when everything else is digital. Paper does not lose power, does not need a login and can be marked up by anyone who finds a gap. The marked-up sheets go back to the studio after the strike and become the first page of the next brief.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->"
  },
  {
   "slug": "vesta-audio-product-launch",
   "title": "Vesta Audio product launch",
   "date": "2026-04-22",
   "categories": [
    "case-studies"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "launch"
   ],
   "image": "assets/images/demo/post-vesta.webp",
   "excerpt": "Staging an intimate hardware reveal with synchronized lighting and broadcast audio feeds.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Vesta Audio asked for a reveal that felt close: 250 guests in the room, a live stream for press, and a product that had to stay under a cloth until the exact second it was lit. The brief came in six weeks before the date and was answered within two working days.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>We built the stage in the warehouse first. The plinth, the lift and the lighting plot were pre-rigged and cabled there, then loaded out in flight cases that rolled straight into the hall at 06:00. Ines Duarte ran the line check at 10:00 and the full technical run-through at 14:00, so the presenter rehearsed against the real cues.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>At 19:00 the cloth came off on a single cue called by Tomasz, the light came up on the plinth and the stream feed switched at the same moment. By 23:30 the hall was struck and handed back swept. The debrief report reached Vesta five days later.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->"
  },
  {
   "slug": "halden-engineering-awards-night",
   "title": "Halden Engineering awards night",
   "date": "2026-02-18",
   "categories": [
    "case-studies"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "awards"
   ],
   "image": "assets/images/demo/post-halden.webp",
   "excerpt": "Managing 420 seated guests, custom stage lecterns and rapid award pacing.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Halden Engineering recognises its people once a year, in categories that range from site safety to apprenticeship of the year. The night had to feel unhurried for the winners and still finish on time for the four hundred and twenty people at the tables.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Priya Raman wrote the run sheet around the pauses: the walk to the stage, the handshake, the photograph. Each category got a fixed slot and a fixed light state, and Kenji Arata programmed the plot so that the room changed colour only twice. Nothing was improvised after the 14:00 rehearsal.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Doors opened at 18:30, the show started at 19:00 and the last award was presented eight minutes ahead of the sheet. The strike crew led by Lucas Weber had the room cleared before midnight.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->"
  },
  {
   "slug": "northline-logistics-annual-kick-off",
   "title": "Northline Logistics annual kick-off",
   "date": "2025-10-09",
   "categories": [
    "case-studies"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "kick-off"
   ],
   "image": "assets/images/demo/kickoff-hall.webp",
   "excerpt": "Seated plenary staging, wide projection and live show calling for 900 guests.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Northline Logistics brings nine hundred people into one hall for a single morning each year. The brief was short: one stage, one screen, everyone able to see and hear, and nothing on the programme that could run over.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Our crew started load-in at 06:00 with the truss and the projection wall, and the first line check was clear by 10:00. A plain stage and a wide screen kept the room calm; the show caller Tomasz Okafor held the plenary to the minute.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The programme ended on time and the hall was empty again before dusk. The report we sent afterwards listed every cue, every change and every item that went back into the cases.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->"
  }
 ],
